WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
Let’s talk about what’s probably the best chance

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.160
we’ll ever get to save the planet

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:05.200
and ourselves.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.200
And we know it’s a mess but for a while there

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.600
nobody was driving or flying, factories shut down.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.120
Our carbon footprint shrank a few sizes.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:30.400
But now — now the global economy

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:31.920
is chugging back to life.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.440
And all those people pushing for a sustainable future?

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.320
Well they’re warning we can’t go back to business as usual.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.000
So how did the pandemic affect the environment?

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.600
And is it possible to bring back the economy

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.000
without killing the planet?

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.720
With COVID-19 the Earth basically caught a break.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.280
And maybe we have too.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:02.800
For starters the global shutdown

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.840
did a lot of environmental good.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.160
People noticed.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.000
It happened fast.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.800
Almost overnight cities like Beijing and New Delhi

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.640
which usually choke on smog

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.120
were able to enjoy baby blue skies.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.760
Bees could smell flowers and find food more easily

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.560
because of less car fumes.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.360
Deserted streets also meant some wild animals

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.480
made a bit of a comeback.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.800
Though the return of swans and dolphins to Venice

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:41.360
well, that was fake.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.640
But still…

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.920
Over the last decade CO2 emissions have been rising

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.960
around 1% every year from things like cars

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.760
planes and factories.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.440
But all of that activity ground to a halt during the pandemic.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.040
And experts think that by early April

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:12.080
global CO2 emissions had decreased by around 17%.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.760
But it’s not all been rosy.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:28.480
Billions of us have been told to wear gloves and masks

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.000
wear more protective gear.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.320
And it’s all piling up on the mountains of garbage

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.120
we’re already dealing with.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.840
Animals that were protected because of tourism

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.680
and the money that came with it

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.160
suddenly became targets for poachers.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.440
And the world lost more rainforests.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.760
In March it was more than twice the monthly average.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:20.720
That standstill is now starting to

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:22.840
look more like a slow shuffle.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:24.880
Lockdowns are lifting, economies are

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.880
powering back up again and emissions are returning

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:30.040
to pre-pandemic levels.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:33.320
Which is worrying because even before this crisis

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:35.600
we had ambitious climate goals.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:36.640
And we’re behind.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.560
Right now the rise in global temperature

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:43.400
is around 1 degree Celsius above pre-industrial levels.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.160
And we’re already feeling it.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.000
There’ve been more wildfires, longer droughts.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.480
The storms are crazy.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:53.280
Ice caps are melting, sea levels are rising.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.680
But we’re on track to hit a 1.5 degree rise by 2030

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:01.640
and soar past that if emissions keep going up.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.960
Now even half a degree may not sound like much

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.160
but it’s a world of difference.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.520
At a 1.5 degree rise the Arctic Ocean would be ice-free

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.360
at least once every 100 years. 

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.400
At 2 degrees  that’s once every 10. 

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:19.880
At 1.5 degrees sea levels would be 10 cm lower

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:21.480
by the end of this century

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.720
than they’d be with a 2 degrees hike.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.920
Coral reefs, well, they’re in bad shape regardless.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.040
To stop that happening we need to

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.520
get carbon levels down.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:32.880
A growing number of countries and companies

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.720
are signing up to the goal of zero carbon by 2050.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.040
That means cutting emissions but also finding ways

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.280
to remove carbon from the atmosphere

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.560
until the net gain is zero.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:47.280
And to do it in 30 years.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.120
But these aren’t normal times.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.880
Which is why this is seen as an opportunity.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:06.640
What if we don’t fully go back to our old ways?

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:09.160
What if, as people in the climate-change business

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:12.080
are saying, we rethink how we recover from

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:13.600
this economic meltdown.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:17.280
Cities like Milan, Athens and London already have plans

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:21.360
to reallocate street space from cars to cycling and walking.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:46.600
As in politicians resistant to green policies

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:49.280
which for climate scientists is a problem.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:51.560
Because what they all say — over and over

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:55.440
is that to stop climate change we have to think big.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.760
Azhar is talking about gross domestic product.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:22.160
Which is the total value of the goods and services

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:25.600
produced by a country usually measured over a year.

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.160
The more GDP grows

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:29.520
the healthier that economy is seen to be.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:30.680
And that model has lifted

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:33.800
hundreds of millions of people out of poverty.

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:37.240
But the cost to the planet isn’t factored into GDP

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:39.160
things like pollution, climate change

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:41.280
and the planet’s resources.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:44.560
It’s why economists say that model isn’t sustainable.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:10.760
But a lot of people say we don’t have to go there.

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:12.840
Climate activists and economists are pointing

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:16.040
to trillions of dollars on the table right now

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:17.280
that governments have earmarked

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.000
to revive economies.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.000
It’s a huge amount of emergency cash.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:58.040
The point they’re making is some of that money

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:00.320
being used to recover from the pandemic

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:03.680
could be used to avoid an even bigger catastrophe.

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:31.840
Obviously nobody wants that to happen.

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:34.200
And right now there’s money and opportunity

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:35.760
to make sure it doesn’t

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:38.320
on a scale none of us have ever seen.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:40.920
So you kind of have to wonder whether this is

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:44.880
our last best chance to get it right.

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:48.880
There’s plenty going on in the world

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:51.560
so if there’s a story in the news that’s complicated

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:53.880
and you want us to explain it let us know.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:56.440
and you want us to explain it let us know.

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:58.760
and make sure to subscribe to our YouTube channel

